  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 401 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nThis Weekend into Next Week: Storm Chances Return\n\nUpper level ridging will become more pronounced this weekend as high \npressure strengthens over the southern Rockies. Before the ridge has \na stronger influence over the Upper Midwest, another very nice day \nis expected for today with highs once again in the 70s to low 80s. \nWith northwesterly flow aloft, some elevated smoke from wildfires is \nexpected to move over the area starting tonight and could last into \nthe day on Saturday. Impacts from the smoke at the surface are not \nexpected as the wind should remain out of the south to southwest. \nRain showers or thunderstorms are not expected during the day, but a \nshower or storm may approach Taylor/Clark counties late tonight into \nSaturday morning.\n\nHeight rises will occur on Saturday into Sunday and strong WAA will \noccur in the low levels this weekend. Moisture throughout the column \nwill be on the increase this weekend which should result in off and \non chances for showers and thunderstorms, with the highest chances \non Sunday afternoon into the evening. The potential is still there \nfor strong to severe storms across portions of the area on Sunday, \nwith the best potential across Wisconsin at this time. The axis for \nwhere the potential for severe storms will likely change some since \nwe are still three days out and will be highly dependent on how far \nnortheast the low level thermal ridge progresses. For areas that are \nmore underneath the thermal ridge, a capping inversion around H800 \nshould lead to much lesser threat for severe storms whereas areas \nfurther northeast will have less capping. For the areas in the more \nuncapped environment, sufficient instability and shear should be in \nplace for some storms to become severe. The primary hazard seems to \nbe damaging wind gusts, but large hail and to a lesser extent a \ntornado threat will also be possible. Thunderstorms may continue \ninto the morning on Monday but the severe storm threat should \ndecrease overnight.\n\nSunday and Monday: Summer Heat Return\n\nHot conditions are still likely to occur on Sunday and Monday as the \naforementioned ridge influences the weather pattern across the \ncentral CONUS. H850 temperatures should be able to reach the mid 20s \nCelsius on both of these days. The increase in surface moisture with \nforecast dew points in the upper 60s to mid 70s will result in heat \nindex values up to 10 degrees warmer than the air temperature. \nConfidence continues to increase in the potential for heat index \nvalues to reach or exceed 100 degrees on both Sunday and Monday.\nThere is still some uncertainty in extent of hottest \ntemperatures as cloud cover combined with convection could lead \nto temperatures being a few degrees cooler than currently \nanticipated. Have issued an Extreme Heat Watch where highest\ndewpoint temperatures keep confidence for reaching Extreme Heat\nWarning at least 50% for parts of southeast Minnesota and\nnortheast Iowa. Will require additional fine tuning as forecast\nhour nears as well as Heat Advisory to the northeast is likely.\n\n\nTroughing may become more pronounced over the Great Lakes region \nduring the middle of next week which should weaken the ridge and \npush it a back further to the west. Warm summer time conditions are \nstill expected the rest of the week even though the ridge may not \nhave as much of an influence over the Upper Mississippi River \nValley.",
